WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:04.760
I'm Jenna Fisher.

2
00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:06.060
And I'm Angela Kinsey.

3
00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:07.340
We were on The Office together.

4
00:00:07.580 --> 00:00:08.600
And we're best friends.

5
00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:12.420
And now we're doing the Ultimate Office rewatch podcast just for you.

6
00:00:12.560 --> 00:00:19.400
Each week, we will break down an episode of The Office and give exclusive behind-the-scenes stories that only two people who were there can tell you.

7
00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:20.560
We're The Office Ladies.

8
00:00:23.960 --> 00:00:24.780
Hi there.

9
00:00:25.460 --> 00:00:26.980
Today's episode is China.

10
00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:29.440
It's Season 7, Episode 10.

11
00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:31.320
Episode 10.

12
00:00:31.620 --> 00:00:34.060
Yeah, we're more than a third of the way through Season 7.

13
00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:35.240
Oh my gosh.

14
00:00:35.240 --> 00:00:41.540
This episode was written by Halstead Sullivan and Warren Lieberstein, and it was directed by Action!

15
00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:43.280
Charles McDougal!

16
00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:44.300
That's right.

17
00:00:44.840 --> 00:00:46.540
Here is your summary.

18
00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:56.880
As owner of the building, Dwight has made numerous cutbacks to electricity use, toilet paper that has everyone in the office pretty grumpy.

19
00:00:57.360 --> 00:00:57.780
Mm-hmm.

20
00:00:58.240 --> 00:01:04.940
As office administrator, Pam goes toe-to-toe with Dwight, threatening to move Dunder Mifflin to a new office space.

21
00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:05.980
Hmm.

22
00:01:06.700 --> 00:01:12.100
Meanwhile, Michael and Oscar find themselves competing for the title of smartest in the office.

23
00:01:12.220 --> 00:01:13.620
That was my favorite!

24
00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:14.620
Me too.

25
00:01:15.400 --> 00:01:20.740
Because it appears that Michael, not Oscar, actually knows more about China.

26
00:01:21.580 --> 00:01:26.920
Finally, Daryl regrets giving Andy his phone number because Andy will not stop texting him.

27
00:01:26.920 --> 00:01:28.920
He is blowing up that text thread.

28
00:01:29.160 --> 00:01:29.340
Yeah.

29
00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:35.900
Well, fast fact number one, this episode aired on December 2nd, 2010.

30
00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:39.540
It was viewed by 7.31 million people.

31
00:01:40.140 --> 00:01:43.160
And I remembered what I was doing when this episode came out.

32
00:01:43.480 --> 00:01:43.960
What?

33
00:01:43.960 --> 00:01:48.220
I was in Detroit, and we were filming The Giant Mechanical Man.

34
00:01:48.220 --> 00:01:50.400
It's the movie that Lee wrote and directed.

35
00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:53.180
I was in it with Chris Messina and Topher Grace.

36
00:01:53.440 --> 00:01:55.760
I got lots of photos from you on that set.

37
00:01:55.920 --> 00:01:58.520
And the biggest parka I had ever seen.

38
00:01:58.520 --> 00:01:59.000
Yes!

39
00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:01.960
I was like, you took the Detroit weather seriously.

40
00:02:02.460 --> 00:02:05.280
I mean, I think the first day we got there, it snowed.

41
00:02:05.480 --> 00:02:12.180
And there's this scene we were shooting at the zoo because my character works at a zoo in the movie,

42
00:02:12.180 --> 00:02:14.940
and I have a meet-cute with Chris Messina.

43
00:02:15.060 --> 00:02:15.960
It's very cute.

44
00:02:16.140 --> 00:02:21.060
And it started like a flurry of snow falling as we met.

45
00:02:21.180 --> 00:02:23.620
It was so amazing and romantic.

46
00:02:23.820 --> 00:02:25.540
And this was a really low-budget movie,

47
00:02:25.540 --> 00:02:29.180
so there was no way we could have brought in snow machines to make it happen.

48
00:02:29.360 --> 00:02:31.800
So you were so excited you got a little bit of that weather magic.

49
00:02:32.040 --> 00:02:32.380
Yes!

50
00:02:32.500 --> 00:02:37.040
It was like we got this big-budget-looking moment, and it was just the sky.

51
00:02:37.480 --> 00:02:40.700
Well, the photo you sent me, I'll never forget because your coat,

52
00:02:40.700 --> 00:02:43.120
I guess it's the first time I'd ever seen a coat like this,

53
00:02:43.160 --> 00:02:45.320
but now I'm like, I know they're common.

54
00:02:45.500 --> 00:02:47.380
It looked like you were wearing a sleeping bag.

55
00:02:47.500 --> 00:02:47.720
Yes!

56
00:02:47.840 --> 00:02:49.200
It like went all the way to the ground.

57
00:02:49.460 --> 00:02:50.600
It was so cold.

58
00:02:51.260 --> 00:02:57.960
But lady, the reason why I bring this up is because we were trading emails during this time,

59
00:02:58.520 --> 00:03:00.740
and I found some of these emails.

60
00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:01.200
Oh, yeah?

61
00:03:01.600 --> 00:03:03.820
Because, you know, in our book, Office BFFs,

62
00:03:03.900 --> 00:03:07.420
we talk about how we stay in touch now through voice messages.

63
00:03:07.820 --> 00:03:08.120
Right.

64
00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:10.340
But this was before we were doing that.

65
00:03:10.340 --> 00:03:12.620
We were still typing it all out to each other.

66
00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:13.580
Long emails.

67
00:03:13.820 --> 00:03:14.660
So long.

68
00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:15.320
So long.

69
00:03:15.420 --> 00:03:17.680
We would write each other like a journal entry,

70
00:03:17.920 --> 00:03:19.820
but better than my actual journal entries.

71
00:03:19.940 --> 00:03:20.620
Don't worry, Sam.

72
00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:21.260
It was better.

73
00:03:22.980 --> 00:03:27.280
I remember like sitting down and writing you like a small novel about my whole week.

74
00:03:27.460 --> 00:03:29.680
Well, you did that, and I found it.

75
00:03:30.180 --> 00:03:33.360
Here's what you were doing the week of December 2nd, 2010.

76
00:03:34.140 --> 00:03:36.720
You had bought your house in the spring.

77
00:03:36.940 --> 00:03:37.120
Yeah.

78
00:03:37.120 --> 00:03:39.440
But you had not had a chance to nest yet.

79
00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:41.780
No, because I was working when I got my house.

80
00:03:41.920 --> 00:03:41.980
Yeah.

81
00:03:42.100 --> 00:03:45.280
So you were using this hiatus to fully nest,

82
00:03:45.280 --> 00:03:49.720
and you wrote me an email announcing a very exciting home purchase.

83
00:03:49.980 --> 00:03:51.500
Do you remember what it was?

84
00:03:51.500 --> 00:03:52.320
I do.

85
00:03:52.900 --> 00:03:55.180
It was a treadmill.

86
00:03:55.720 --> 00:03:57.260
I was so excited.

87
00:03:57.260 --> 00:04:01.060
You guys, I looked for a long time, and I bought one at Sears,

88
00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:07.340
and it promptly became the place where I hung my sweaters and put boxes on it.

89
00:04:07.340 --> 00:04:11.380
But you were so excited about it in your email.

90
00:04:11.460 --> 00:04:17.180
You really believed that this was going to kickstart a new habit in your life.

91
00:04:17.320 --> 00:04:18.240
It's such a joke.

92
00:04:18.400 --> 00:04:20.160
For any of you guys that follow me on Instagram,

93
00:04:20.460 --> 00:04:25.140
you know that like I would just post photo after photo of what all I was using my treadmill for,

94
00:04:25.360 --> 00:04:26.120
other than exercise.

95
00:04:26.440 --> 00:04:26.680
Yes.

96
00:04:26.940 --> 00:04:29.440
It's a great place to dry clothes that, you know,

97
00:04:29.580 --> 00:04:31.540
you want to take out of the dryer a little bit early.

98
00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:33.380
So that's real good.

99
00:04:33.500 --> 00:04:34.780
But you don't have it anymore.

100
00:04:34.780 --> 00:04:38.040
No, I finally just gave it to my neighbor because I was like, who am I kidding?

101
00:04:38.660 --> 00:04:40.140
How long did you have it?

102
00:04:40.180 --> 00:04:42.980
It was like, I mean, five, eight years?

103
00:04:43.140 --> 00:04:47.420
Yeah, it took me like a decade to give up on it, to realize it was just storage.

104
00:04:47.600 --> 00:04:49.740
But then I reimagined the space.

105
00:04:49.880 --> 00:04:50.120
Yes.

106
00:04:50.320 --> 00:04:51.820
I made myself a little nook.

107
00:04:51.940 --> 00:04:53.460
And it is so cute.

108
00:04:53.620 --> 00:04:55.780
And bringing it back to the book,

109
00:04:55.920 --> 00:04:59.900
you spent a lot of time working on office BFFs from your new nook

110
00:05:00.360 --> 00:05:02.580
that went in the place of this treadmill.

111
00:05:02.780 --> 00:05:04.260
The treadmill slash storage.

112
00:05:04.780 --> 00:05:05.040
Area.

113
00:05:05.460 --> 00:05:08.280
But this email really brought back memories for me

114
00:05:08.280 --> 00:05:11.440
because I just remember this whole treadmill thing.

115
00:05:11.740 --> 00:05:13.800
I was so, I was so determined.

116
00:05:14.180 --> 00:05:16.420
I don't know what the moral of that story is.

117
00:05:16.480 --> 00:05:17.160
I don't know.

118
00:05:17.340 --> 00:05:19.380
But maybe if you haven't used your treadmill.

119
00:05:19.660 --> 00:05:21.840
In a long time, just get real with yourself.

120
00:05:22.120 --> 00:05:22.300
Yeah.

121
00:05:22.440 --> 00:05:24.380
And put in a cozy reading nook instead.

122
00:05:24.620 --> 00:05:25.240
It's okay.

123
00:05:25.240 --> 00:05:27.180
We give you permission today.

124
00:05:27.960 --> 00:05:29.220
We're talking to you.

125
00:05:29.320 --> 00:05:30.780
You know who you are.

126
00:05:31.640 --> 00:05:32.360
All right.

127
00:05:32.540 --> 00:05:33.060
Moving on.

128
00:05:33.220 --> 00:05:34.420
Fast fact number two.

129
00:05:35.080 --> 00:05:37.440
We got a fan question from Haley H.

130
00:05:37.540 --> 00:05:38.400
in Alberta, Canada.

131
00:05:38.820 --> 00:05:42.980
This was such a great episode, but also a very unusual premise.

132
00:05:42.980 --> 00:05:46.120
What was the inspiration for this episode?

133
00:05:47.200 --> 00:05:51.620
Well, Haley, we reached out to Warren and Halstead, who wrote this episode.

134
00:05:51.620 --> 00:05:57.840
And Halstead gave us some great intel on the inspiration for all of the various storylines

135
00:05:57.840 --> 00:05:58.620
in this episode.

136
00:05:58.960 --> 00:06:04.200
He said that the writers had been tossing around the idea of a China episode for a while

137
00:06:04.200 --> 00:06:10.300
because China had been rapidly rising as the next superpower, but that the real seed of

138
00:06:10.300 --> 00:06:15.200
this idea sprung from a discussion in the writers' room after they had watched the 2008

139
00:06:15.200 --> 00:06:17.540
Olympic opening ceremony in Beijing.

140
00:06:18.280 --> 00:06:23.900
Because he remembered they were all just talking about just the sheer number of performers and

141
00:06:23.900 --> 00:06:27.020
how they were all moving with precision and the drummers.

142
00:06:27.440 --> 00:06:32.840
And, you know, Warren told us that in their first draft, they had Michael watching the 2008

143
00:06:32.840 --> 00:06:36.020
Olympic opening ceremony on YouTube with Aaron.

144
00:06:36.420 --> 00:06:39.140
Oh, you know, I did read that in an earlier draft.

145
00:06:39.280 --> 00:06:39.480
Yeah.

146
00:06:39.660 --> 00:06:39.940
Mm-hmm.

147
00:06:40.420 --> 00:06:44.080
But it took him a little while to figure out how could this become an episode.

148
00:06:44.280 --> 00:06:44.640
Mm-hmm.

149
00:06:45.120 --> 00:06:52.240
He said eventually the writers came up with this really fun idea of Oscar being known as actually.

150
00:06:52.840 --> 00:06:54.180
Yes, I love that.

151
00:06:54.700 --> 00:07:02.160
And that kind of grew into him being considered the smartest in the office and what would happen

152
00:07:02.160 --> 00:07:03.940
if Michael bested him.

153
00:07:04.140 --> 00:07:09.380
And then they joined the idea of focusing on China and then focusing on Michael becoming

154
00:07:09.380 --> 00:07:12.840
the smartest in the office to come up with this episode.

155
00:07:13.320 --> 00:07:17.240
But they also wanted to explore Dwight as the building owner.

156
00:07:17.460 --> 00:07:22.160
And since Pam was the new office administrator, they thought it would be really fun to have

157
00:07:22.160 --> 00:07:24.020
a showdown between the two of them.

158
00:07:24.300 --> 00:07:28.720
And it really sets up some future episodes, like even Classy Christmas, you know?

159
00:07:28.840 --> 00:07:28.940
Mm-hmm.

160
00:07:28.940 --> 00:07:30.780
She actually plans an amazing Christmas.

161
00:07:31.180 --> 00:07:36.860
They also like the idea of Pam having to deal with this rather than Michael, you know?

162
00:07:37.020 --> 00:07:37.140
Yeah.

163
00:07:37.240 --> 00:07:42.140
Like the whole banner on the side of the building that Pam has to do this.

164
00:07:43.040 --> 00:07:49.000
And then as far as the Andy and Daryl texting storyline, they said they really wanted to deepen

165
00:07:49.000 --> 00:07:52.120
the relationship between Andy and Daryl.

166
00:07:52.120 --> 00:07:54.520
That kind of started when they made their band.

167
00:07:54.800 --> 00:07:55.000
Mm-hmm.

168
00:07:55.000 --> 00:07:56.520
They liked those two together.

169
00:07:56.900 --> 00:08:01.020
They said texting was relatively new back then.

170
00:08:01.080 --> 00:08:01.600
Oh, my God.

171
00:08:01.620 --> 00:08:02.600
We're so old.

172
00:08:03.160 --> 00:08:08.360
The iPhone had just come out in 2007, and we finally had this whole keyboard that we could

173
00:08:08.360 --> 00:08:08.940
text from.

174
00:08:09.060 --> 00:08:10.960
You didn't have to do, like, just the multiple.

175
00:08:10.960 --> 00:08:12.360
One, one, one, two, two, two.

176
00:08:12.540 --> 00:08:14.840
Yes, all that, if people even know what we're talking about.

177
00:08:14.880 --> 00:08:14.980
I know.

178
00:08:14.980 --> 00:08:15.780
Because we're so old.

179
00:08:15.860 --> 00:08:16.180
I know.

180
00:08:16.180 --> 00:08:21.580
So they said something that was very popular back at the time was this new idea of texting

181
00:08:21.580 --> 00:08:22.300
etiquette.

182
00:08:23.080 --> 00:08:25.900
So that's what they decided to throw into that relationship.

183
00:08:26.480 --> 00:08:32.760
This is so funny to me because, well, in the last few years, we got my mom not a smartphone,

184
00:08:33.140 --> 00:08:33.940
but, like, an iPad.

185
00:08:34.380 --> 00:08:34.720
Mm-hmm.

186
00:08:34.720 --> 00:08:35.860
And so now she texts.

187
00:08:35.960 --> 00:08:37.280
She's never texted in her life.

188
00:08:37.280 --> 00:08:38.320
She's in her 80s.

189
00:08:38.500 --> 00:08:38.740
Okay.

190
00:08:38.880 --> 00:08:44.340
And just, like, watching my mom's learning curve on texting is so adorable.

191
00:08:44.920 --> 00:08:49.120
And, like, you know, like, the moment your parent discovers an emoji.

192
00:08:49.440 --> 00:08:50.360
Right, yes.

193
00:08:50.540 --> 00:08:51.440
It's so cute.

194
00:08:51.720 --> 00:08:55.880
Well, I guess all the different generations have their own texting etiquette.

195
00:08:56.040 --> 00:08:58.780
Our generation writes full sentences with punctuation.

196
00:08:58.980 --> 00:08:59.320
I know.

197
00:08:59.480 --> 00:09:06.220
But apparently that's really annoying to, is it the millennials hate that, or is it Gen Z?

198
00:09:06.220 --> 00:09:06.270
I don't know.

199
00:09:06.270 --> 00:09:07.560
We're looking at you guys in the booth.

200
00:09:07.560 --> 00:09:09.740
Also, like, somebody hates emojis.

201
00:09:10.160 --> 00:09:10.680
Yeah.

202
00:09:10.880 --> 00:09:11.540
What is it?

203
00:09:11.540 --> 00:09:15.300
I know for me, if you put a period at the end of the sentence, that means you're mad

204
00:09:15.300 --> 00:09:16.000
at the person.

205
00:09:16.200 --> 00:09:16.520
Oh!

206
00:09:16.840 --> 00:09:17.140
See?

207
00:09:17.280 --> 00:09:18.040
That's what I mean.

208
00:09:18.200 --> 00:09:20.460
We put a period all the time, Cassie.

209
00:09:20.460 --> 00:09:24.080
And I know a bunch of emojis is an old person thing.

210
00:09:24.240 --> 00:09:27.120
Like, a bunch of, like, the crying emoji for laughing is a...

211
00:09:27.120 --> 00:09:27.900
Oh, that's me.

212
00:09:27.960 --> 00:09:28.780
I do it all the time.

213
00:09:28.780 --> 00:09:31.660
What do you do if you're laughing so hard you're crying?

214
00:09:31.660 --> 00:09:32.180
I don't know.

215
00:09:32.220 --> 00:09:32.940
I'm 38.

216
00:09:33.100 --> 00:09:34.580
Check in with someone who isn't me.

217
00:09:35.360 --> 00:09:37.600
Well, the other thing...

218
00:09:37.600 --> 00:09:41.940
Also, I guess to write out full words is considered ancient as well.

219
00:09:42.080 --> 00:09:43.440
There's all these abbreviations.

220
00:09:43.700 --> 00:09:45.640
That's also read as, like, passive-aggressive full.

221
00:09:45.820 --> 00:09:46.040
Yeah.

222
00:09:46.260 --> 00:09:52.220
I write, see you soon, exclamation point, and I write out every word, but I feel like

223
00:09:52.220 --> 00:09:57.100
someone else writes the letter C, the letter U, and soon with no period or...

224
00:09:57.100 --> 00:09:58.060
In lowercase two.

225
00:09:58.360 --> 00:09:58.560
Right.

226
00:09:58.680 --> 00:09:59.060
Oh, that's right.

227
00:09:59.120 --> 00:10:00.440
Don't capitalize anything.

228
00:10:00.580 --> 00:10:01.060
My gosh.

229
00:10:01.260 --> 00:10:02.660
Isabel just taught me a new one.

230
00:10:02.660 --> 00:10:04.600
And she had a cold bug, and I was like, how are you feeling today?

231
00:10:04.760 --> 00:10:06.960
And she said, I'm going to get it wrong.

232
00:10:07.140 --> 00:10:08.460
She said, med.

233
00:10:09.220 --> 00:10:09.920
What's that mean?

234
00:10:10.000 --> 00:10:10.540
I said, med?

235
00:10:11.320 --> 00:10:13.200
You know, she's like, you're so-so.

236
00:10:13.440 --> 00:10:14.160
You're like, oh, yeah.

237
00:10:14.420 --> 00:10:15.200
Yeah, she's feeling med.

238
00:10:15.440 --> 00:10:15.780
Mid.

239
00:10:15.880 --> 00:10:16.700
Oh, so I got it wrong.

240
00:10:16.840 --> 00:10:17.060
I got it wrong.

241
00:10:17.260 --> 00:10:18.820
I thought you were saying meh.

242
00:10:19.140 --> 00:10:19.720
I know.

243
00:10:19.920 --> 00:10:20.920
I got it wrong.

244
00:10:21.000 --> 00:10:24.740
She's feeling mid, which is, like, you know, in the middle, like so-so.

245
00:10:25.240 --> 00:10:25.600
Oh.

246
00:10:25.980 --> 00:10:26.580
I know.

247
00:10:26.940 --> 00:10:30.340
What's also not good, like, a song that's mid would be bad.

248
00:10:30.780 --> 00:10:31.140
Okay.

249
00:10:31.140 --> 00:10:32.880
But let me ask you this question.

250
00:10:33.100 --> 00:10:37.120
Let's say Angela and I took a class on young people texting.

251
00:10:37.120 --> 00:10:38.140
Nothing would make me happier.

252
00:10:38.180 --> 00:10:38.500
Go on.

253
00:10:38.680 --> 00:10:44.380
But if we did, and then we started texting that way, would it be annoying?

254
00:10:44.680 --> 00:10:46.420
Like, are you supposed to stay in your lane?

255
00:10:46.580 --> 00:10:47.820
Just stay in your generation?

256
00:10:48.740 --> 00:10:49.400
I don't know.

257
00:10:49.460 --> 00:10:50.000
But you know what?

258
00:10:50.160 --> 00:10:52.200
