1033
00:43:30.750 --> 00:43:31.410
That's all.

1034
00:43:31.870 --> 00:43:33.250
What is that, like five words?

1035
00:43:33.450 --> 00:43:34.330
It's not a big deal.

1036
00:43:35.110 --> 00:43:36.870
Can I use your camera, please?

1037
00:43:37.290 --> 00:43:38.510
Add that extra please in there.

1038
00:43:38.650 --> 00:43:39.190
Still not hard.

1039
00:43:40.930 --> 00:43:43.630
She just always changes the settings and...

1040
00:43:48.330 --> 00:43:49.330
Isn't that so funny?

1041
00:43:49.330 --> 00:43:53.990
So when you go rewatch the scene, I want you to look for Jim's reactions to the photos

1042
00:43:53.990 --> 00:43:54.830
that Pam's showing.

1043
00:43:55.170 --> 00:43:57.110
It's not about the office space.

1044
00:43:57.250 --> 00:44:02.310
It's about the fact this whole time Jim thinks that Pam took his camera and didn't ask.

1045
00:44:02.370 --> 00:44:04.610
And I was so curious when I watched the scene.

1046
00:44:04.750 --> 00:44:07.190
I thought, Jim is acting kind of weird.

1047
00:44:07.630 --> 00:44:07.930
Yeah.

1048
00:44:08.090 --> 00:44:09.050
And that's why.

1049
00:44:09.050 --> 00:44:13.630
It's because he's annoyed because Pam takes his camera and changes all the settings.

1050
00:44:13.930 --> 00:44:19.370
This is a clear case of Jim thinking of it as his camera and Pam thinking of it as their camera.

1051
00:44:19.950 --> 00:44:20.470
Right?

1052
00:44:20.770 --> 00:44:21.150
Yeah.

1053
00:44:22.210 --> 00:44:25.870
Well, Pam is really going to sell this new workspace to the whole office.

1054
00:44:25.870 --> 00:44:28.170
She's passing around the photos to everyone.

1055
00:44:28.430 --> 00:44:33.070
It kind of sounds too good to be true because it seems to have something for everyone.

1056
00:44:33.310 --> 00:44:36.610
It's next to an outback, so it always smells like steak.

1057
00:44:36.830 --> 00:44:40.330
It's next to a nail place that makes Kelly very happy.

1058
00:44:41.010 --> 00:44:44.830
Later, one of my favorite things in the stairwell is when Jim says,

1059
00:44:45.350 --> 00:44:46.970
wait, so it's not near a bike path?

1060
00:44:47.050 --> 00:44:47.630
I know.

1061
00:44:47.630 --> 00:44:51.370
Like she's clearly just found something about it for everyone.

1062
00:44:51.830 --> 00:44:55.510
Warren shared with us that there was a different version where Pam is going around

1063
00:44:55.510 --> 00:44:59.810
talking to everyone about this great new office, and Pam approaches Toby,

1064
00:44:59.970 --> 00:45:01.670
and this is how the scene would have played out.

1065
00:45:02.030 --> 00:45:07.910
Toby tells Pam that he's resistant to move because moving is one of the most five stressful

1066
00:45:07.910 --> 00:45:13.210
things in life, along with work, loneliness, debt, and divorce, which he has experienced

1067
00:45:13.210 --> 00:45:15.370
way too often in a myriad of ways.

1068
00:45:16.230 --> 00:45:21.830
But if this new office had a window, then maybe he could get the cactus that he always wanted.

1069
00:45:22.610 --> 00:45:28.050
And Warren said, oh, Toby, it was really difficult to turn in a script without at least one sad

1070
00:45:28.050 --> 00:45:28.830
Toby remark.

1071
00:45:31.430 --> 00:45:36.790
Randy told us that those photos were photos taken by Michael Gallenberg, and they were

1072
00:45:36.790 --> 00:45:38.610
actually scouting photos.

1073
00:45:39.370 --> 00:45:44.970
When we were shooting The Sting, we were looking at locations that maybe we could go on location

1074
00:45:44.970 --> 00:45:51.630
for those scenes where Dwight and Michael and Jim run into Danny Cordray, and I guess this

1075
00:45:51.630 --> 00:45:53.610
was one of the spots that they scouted.

1076
00:45:53.670 --> 00:45:57.470
So we just had these old photos, and that's what Pam is showing everybody.

1077
00:45:57.870 --> 00:46:01.970
Well, I think we should title this next scene actually in the break room.

1078
00:46:02.150 --> 00:46:03.510
Oh, I had a different title.

1079
00:46:03.730 --> 00:46:04.270
What was it?

1080
00:46:04.370 --> 00:46:06.250
I called it Brains in the Break Room.

1081
00:46:06.450 --> 00:46:07.850
Oh, that's probably better.

1082
00:46:09.030 --> 00:46:14.770
So everyone's in the break room, and Oscar's going to walk in, and Kelly's really like teasing him.

1083
00:46:15.210 --> 00:46:18.450
And Kelly and Ryan are like, oh, actually, Oscar, actually.

1084
00:46:18.770 --> 00:46:24.430
And that's when we have this Jim talking head where he says Oscar is known as, quote, actually

1085
00:46:24.430 --> 00:46:30.050
because of the way he inserts himself into every conversation to add facts or correct grammar.

1086
00:46:30.370 --> 00:46:34.650
And then there's a montage of Oscar walking by going, actually, actually.

1087
00:46:34.650 --> 00:46:35.670
Mm-hmm.

1088
00:46:35.930 --> 00:46:38.890
But then Oscar's going to ask Michael to go to coffee.

1089
00:46:39.330 --> 00:46:40.370
He's like, you know what, Michael?

1090
00:46:40.490 --> 00:46:42.850
I'd like to hear some more of those China facts.

1091
00:46:43.330 --> 00:46:44.230
This is a trap.

1092
00:46:44.690 --> 00:46:44.850
Yeah.

1093
00:46:45.070 --> 00:46:46.790
Andy, Jim, everyone, they're freaking out.

1094
00:46:46.910 --> 00:46:47.710
They're like, whoa, whoa, whoa.

1095
00:46:48.210 --> 00:46:49.610
Michael, this is a trap.

1096
00:46:49.850 --> 00:46:51.230
Oscar's going to set you up.

1097
00:46:51.390 --> 00:46:51.790
Yes.

1098
00:46:52.070 --> 00:46:54.210
Well, Michael has already accepted the invitation.

1099
00:46:54.210 --> 00:46:58.650
So just to be safe, he's going to try to learn everything he can about China.

1100
00:46:58.970 --> 00:47:01.850
And probably geography and math and literature.

1101
00:47:02.070 --> 00:47:02.530
But you know what?

1102
00:47:02.730 --> 00:47:04.130
He's good on politics.

1103
00:47:04.130 --> 00:47:05.090
He's got that down.

1104
00:47:05.390 --> 00:47:06.850
Yeah, apparently.

1105
00:47:07.210 --> 00:47:12.490
And in the meantime, Andy is still trying to text Daryl something good.

1106
00:47:12.950 --> 00:47:17.210
Daryl's at the photocopier, and he gets a text that just says Megan Fox.

1107
00:47:17.610 --> 00:47:21.970
Daryl's going to say, listen, you are one bad text away from getting blocked.

1108
00:47:22.490 --> 00:47:25.930
Ah, but one good text away from a high five, Andy says.

1109
00:47:26.310 --> 00:47:28.030
I really wish we could say this, though.

1110
00:47:28.150 --> 00:47:32.590
I wish that you could just have a rule that you have a limit of bad text.

1111
00:47:32.590 --> 00:47:35.890
And then you could tell someone, listen, you're one text away from being blocked.

1112
00:47:35.930 --> 00:47:36.650
And it's okay.

1113
00:47:36.810 --> 00:47:38.090
It's an accepted rule.

1114
00:47:38.330 --> 00:47:41.570
I often feel overwhelmed by text threads.

1115
00:47:41.810 --> 00:47:42.750
The text threads.

1116
00:47:43.150 --> 00:47:43.530
Yeah.

1117
00:47:44.090 --> 00:47:44.850
It's a lot.

1118
00:47:45.090 --> 00:47:45.430
It's a lot.

1119
00:47:45.570 --> 00:47:47.430
And sometimes you have to scroll way back.

1120
00:47:47.570 --> 00:47:50.750
Someone will make a reference way later, and then you're like, what are they talking about?

1121
00:47:50.750 --> 00:47:51.090
Yeah.

1122
00:47:51.590 --> 00:47:52.270
I know.

1123
00:47:52.790 --> 00:47:59.010
At 11 minutes and 8 seconds, I have a question about this scene, which is, why is Daryl making

1124
00:47:59.010 --> 00:48:01.310
a photocopy without putting the top down?

1125
00:48:01.870 --> 00:48:02.970
It bothered me.

1126
00:48:03.230 --> 00:48:03.850
I don't know.

1127
00:48:03.970 --> 00:48:04.970
I mean, does that even work?

1128
00:48:05.190 --> 00:48:07.550
Can you even make a photocopy if you don't put the top down?

1129
00:48:07.650 --> 00:48:08.610
I tried to Google it.

1130
00:48:08.690 --> 00:48:09.510
I couldn't find out.

1131
00:48:09.590 --> 00:48:10.210
I think you can.

1132
00:48:10.410 --> 00:48:10.750
Really?

1133
00:48:10.910 --> 00:48:11.110
Yeah.

1134
00:48:11.950 --> 00:48:12.350
All right.

1135
00:48:12.470 --> 00:48:17.710
Well, I just, I mean, the light flashing up as he made it.

1136
00:48:17.810 --> 00:48:18.950
It was, it's a pet peeve.

1137
00:48:18.950 --> 00:48:19.710
Disturbing for you.

1138
00:48:19.710 --> 00:48:20.870
Just put the lid down, everybody.

1139
00:48:21.470 --> 00:48:25.830
If I'm with you, will you put the lid down for me, I guess, is what I'm saying.

1140
00:48:26.050 --> 00:48:27.250
Yes, I'll put the lid down.

1141
00:48:27.490 --> 00:48:28.030
Thank you.

1142
00:48:28.290 --> 00:48:31.150
In the kitchen, we're going to have some pirate talk.

1143
00:48:31.770 --> 00:48:32.210
Mm-hmm.

1144
00:48:32.510 --> 00:48:35.690
Dwight walks by, says, parlay, my office, five minutes.

1145
00:48:36.250 --> 00:48:36.510
Yep.

1146
00:48:36.690 --> 00:48:37.450
Pam's like parlay.

1147
00:48:37.570 --> 00:48:38.450
Creed's like pirate code.

1148
00:48:38.530 --> 00:48:39.190
He wants to meet.

1149
00:48:39.830 --> 00:48:40.030
Yeah.

1150
00:48:40.390 --> 00:48:43.870
By the way, Creed cannot speak pirate, but he can understand it.

1151
00:48:43.970 --> 00:48:44.130
Yeah.

1152
00:48:44.650 --> 00:48:46.410
Here's what Dwight is going to agree to.

1153
00:48:46.410 --> 00:48:52.270
He's going to agree to stop watering down the soap if Pam agrees not to move.

1154
00:48:52.610 --> 00:48:54.730
Then they have this back and forth.

1155
00:48:55.070 --> 00:48:56.490
That is so good.

1156
00:48:57.670 --> 00:49:00.490
And Jenna, I just wanted to read Pam's final put down.

1157
00:49:00.750 --> 00:49:01.530
Okay, please.

1158
00:49:01.870 --> 00:49:06.730
Dwight has made some horrible, like, predictions about what her future role looked like with Jim,

1159
00:49:07.110 --> 00:49:07.730
with Cece.

1160
00:49:08.410 --> 00:49:10.670
Pam says, that's a sad story.

1161
00:49:10.910 --> 00:49:12.170
I have another one.

1162
00:49:21.830 --> 00:49:30.430
And then the lights go off, and they both have to wave their arms to get them back on.

1163
00:49:30.650 --> 00:49:32.770
It was really hard for me to do that without laughing.

1164
00:49:32.790 --> 00:49:35.790
I think you can even see I'm smiling a little bit at the end of the scene.

1165
00:49:36.370 --> 00:49:40.190
I just like the idea that it's the bank that beats up Dwight.

1166
00:49:41.450 --> 00:49:45.570
Well, you know, in the background of the scene, the whole time this is going on,

1167
00:49:45.850 --> 00:49:53.650
Nate is at a very elaborate machine where he is turning one roll of toilet paper into two rolls

1168
00:49:53.650 --> 00:49:56.070
because he's separating the plies.

1169
00:49:56.630 --> 00:50:00.750
This machine looks like something you would see on a school field trip if you went to,

1170
00:50:00.750 --> 00:50:02.670
like, ye olde farming mine.

1171
00:50:02.910 --> 00:50:03.370
Yes.

1172
00:50:03.510 --> 00:50:07.210
Where you got to, like, churn butter and pick some apples,

1173
00:50:07.210 --> 00:50:08.930
and there'd be this machine in the corner.

1174
00:50:09.290 --> 00:50:12.430
Well, Jenna, we got a fan question from Akankesha in India.

1175
00:50:12.730 --> 00:50:16.450
How on earth did Phil Shea make the toilet paper spool thing?

1176
00:50:16.610 --> 00:50:17.650
Did it actually work?

1177
00:50:17.790 --> 00:50:20.750
Did he give the assignment to an outside person or organization?

1178
00:50:21.050 --> 00:50:22.530
How much did it cost to make?

1179
00:50:22.830 --> 00:50:24.670
I have all the same questions.

1180
00:50:25.030 --> 00:50:28.050
Well, thanks to Randy Cordray, we have the answer.

1181
00:50:28.050 --> 00:50:34.370
This device cost us $3,000 in materials and overtime labor.

1182
00:50:34.910 --> 00:50:37.330
Basically, this was a homemade machine.

1183
00:50:38.030 --> 00:50:44.130
Phil Shea went out and found an old-timey hand crank off of some sort of antique device

1184
00:50:44.130 --> 00:50:49.050
and then built a toilet paper splitting loom.

1185
00:50:49.190 --> 00:50:49.910
You are kidding.

1186
00:50:50.090 --> 00:50:50.610
This was...

1187
00:50:50.610 --> 00:50:51.870
So, they had this built.

1188
00:50:52.050 --> 00:50:52.830
Someone built this.

1189
00:50:52.890 --> 00:50:53.530
It was their job.

1190
00:50:53.530 --> 00:50:53.970
Yes.

1191
00:50:54.250 --> 00:50:57.930
Phil had to go to the engineers at the hand prop room.

1192
00:50:58.030 --> 00:50:58.750
Remember that place?

1193
00:50:59.090 --> 00:51:01.510
And they designed it and made it.

1194
00:51:02.070 --> 00:51:06.890
Randy said, remember Dwight's nutcracker Christmas gift from Secret Santa?

1195
00:51:07.250 --> 00:51:08.610
Oh, they're the same people?

1196
00:51:08.830 --> 00:51:09.130
Yeah.

1197
00:51:09.310 --> 00:51:12.750
This is very similar, too, where they basically said,

1198
00:51:12.990 --> 00:51:15.090
create something that doesn't exist in real life.

1199
00:51:15.090 --> 00:51:16.590
It has to work perfectly.

1200
00:51:16.770 --> 00:51:18.230
And I need it tomorrow.

1201
00:51:18.650 --> 00:51:21.930
And Randy sent us a test video when they were making it.

1202
00:51:22.070 --> 00:51:23.290
It's really cool to watch.

1203
00:51:23.290 --> 00:51:24.310
I'll put it in stories.

1204
00:51:24.810 --> 00:51:28.350
Here they are, Jenna, making this machine.

1205
00:51:28.950 --> 00:51:32.430
Oh, it is incredible.

1206
00:51:32.730 --> 00:51:34.070
It's really fun.

1207
00:51:34.310 --> 00:51:38.950
I have to imagine it's exciting to get an order like this.

1208
00:51:39.830 --> 00:51:41.570
Especially if you love to build things.

1209
00:51:41.750 --> 00:51:41.930
Yeah.

1210
00:51:42.050 --> 00:51:45.010
I mean, my dad would love to take on a challenge like this.

1211
00:51:45.070 --> 00:51:46.110
My dad's an engineer.

1212
00:51:46.310 --> 00:51:47.010
He would love it.

1213
00:51:47.470 --> 00:51:51.790
Halstead and Warren shared with us that there were so many times they would write a joke into a script,

1214
00:51:51.790 --> 00:51:53.750
not even knowing if it was possible.

1215
00:51:53.990 --> 00:51:56.890
And then, lo and behold, this amazing machine would appear.

1216
00:51:57.170 --> 00:52:00.450
And not only did this machine work to de-ply the paper,

1217
00:52:00.610 --> 00:52:05.450
they fixed it so it would be a disaster when Dwight ordered Nate to re-ply it.

1218
00:52:05.450 --> 00:52:06.990
It spins out like crazy.

1219
00:52:07.350 --> 00:52:12.530
And they both shared with us that it truly made the scene that much funnier than they could have ever imagined.

1220
00:52:12.530 --> 00:52:14.450
So they wanted to give Phil a big shout out.

1221
00:52:14.690 --> 00:52:16.030
Yeah, it's pretty amazing.

1222
00:52:16.810 --> 00:52:22.230
Before we move on from the scene, though, Angela, I have one small little background catch for people who like that stuff.

1223
00:52:22.230 --> 00:52:24.330
At 13 minutes and 13 seconds.

1224
00:52:24.570 --> 00:52:24.930
What you got?

1225
00:52:25.190 --> 00:52:27.590
The picture behind Dwight is still tilted.

1226
00:52:28.090 --> 00:52:33.510
So I guess he knows that no one has come in to rob him because his picture is still crooked.

1227
00:52:34.050 --> 00:52:38.030
And also, I noticed a plastic banana in his pencil cup.

1228
00:52:38.370 --> 00:52:39.790
Don't know what that's about, but there it is.

1229
00:52:39.830 --> 00:52:40.530
There you go.

1230
00:52:41.230 --> 00:52:46.710
Well, Michael is being quizzed in preparation for his big SmackDown meeting with Oscar.

1231
00:52:47.470 --> 00:52:48.770
Ryan says, where's Tibet?

1232
00:52:48.990 --> 00:52:49.830
Michael says, pass.

1233
00:52:50.010 --> 00:52:51.990
Ryan says, when was China founded, Michael?

1234
00:52:52.230 --> 00:52:52.570
Pass.

1235
00:52:52.750 --> 00:52:53.770
Jim says, two for two.

1236
00:52:53.910 --> 00:52:54.330
Keep it up.

1237
00:52:54.530 --> 00:52:55.870
Ryan says, who is Mao?

1238
00:52:56.150 --> 00:52:57.310
Michael says, lifeline.

1239
00:52:57.790 --> 00:52:59.990
And Andy goes, damn it, Michael.

1240
00:53:00.290 --> 00:53:03.310
You're moments away from the SmackDown of your life.

1241
00:53:03.810 --> 00:53:07.530
They encourage him to steer the conversation back to something he knows.

1242
00:53:07.670 --> 00:53:09.170
Michael suggests boobs.

1243
00:53:10.110 --> 00:53:12.270
But then he doesn't know anything about boobs.

1244
00:53:12.450 --> 00:53:12.650
No.

1245
00:53:12.850 --> 00:53:16.750
And then Andy says, Michael, I have to tell you something.

1246
00:53:16.750 --> 00:53:18.190
It's from Rocky 2.

1247
00:53:19.250 --> 00:53:20.910
This moment is so funny.

1248
00:53:21.050 --> 00:53:21.890
It's so funny.

1249
00:53:21.890 --> 00:53:23.930
In the shooting draft, this is how it read.

1250
00:53:24.190 --> 00:53:29.890
Andy delivers his Rocky speech like Burgess Meredith, who was the coach.

1251
00:53:30.690 --> 00:53:32.530
Ed kept cracking up Steve.

1252
00:53:32.970 --> 00:53:34.770
Steve could not keep it together.

1253
00:53:34.930 --> 00:53:36.790
And it made it into the bloopers.

1254
00:53:36.790 --> 00:53:37.850
You have to hear it.

1255
00:53:38.210 --> 00:53:40.250
This guy doesn't just want to win, you know.

1256
00:53:40.410 --> 00:53:41.590
He wants to bury you.

1257
00:53:41.790 --> 00:53:43.530
He wants to humiliate you.

1258
00:53:43.710 --> 00:53:45.410
And he wants to prove to the world.

1259
00:53:45.510 --> 00:53:45.610
Wait.

1260
00:53:46.010 --> 00:53:47.010
How long is this going to take?

1261
00:53:47.010 --> 00:53:48.690
I'm like a quarter of the way through.

1262
00:53:56.570 --> 00:53:59.370
I loved it when anyone could make Steve break.

1263
00:53:59.770 --> 00:54:03.730
Now Nate and Dwight are doing a little mom detective work.

1264
00:54:03.890 --> 00:54:10.010
Frankly, they're going out to look for this building that Pam has supposedly found that's way better than Dwight's building.

1265
00:54:10.010 --> 00:54:12.830
But guess what they drive up to?

1266
00:54:13.090 --> 00:54:14.270
It's an empty lot.

1267
00:54:14.570 --> 00:54:16.050
The building's underground.

1268
00:54:16.330 --> 00:54:18.010
That's what Nate says.

1269
00:54:18.870 --> 00:54:20.230
Dwight's like, you know what this means.

1270
00:54:21.690 --> 00:54:25.490
And then Dwight's like, Pam, Pam, Pam.

1271
00:54:25.490 --> 00:54:28.710
And then together they're like, Pam, Pam, Pam.

1272
00:54:29.050 --> 00:54:32.690
And then a woman named Pam just happens to be walking by.

1273
00:54:33.030 --> 00:54:33.630
Yes.

1274
00:54:33.990 --> 00:54:37.930
We got a fan question from Amy Z in Westchester, Pennsylvania.

1275
00:54:38.250 --> 00:54:41.730
How on earth did the extra Pam scene come together?

1276
00:54:41.990 --> 00:54:43.290
Who is this actress?

1277
00:54:43.630 --> 00:54:48.210
From the whole series, I love the line, quote, I've been known to bend the truth.

1278
00:54:48.450 --> 00:54:49.990
It's my absolute favorite.

1279
00:54:50.330 --> 00:54:53.030
It is one of my favorite too for so many reasons.

1280
00:54:53.030 --> 00:54:57.470
First of all, if a perfect stranger says to you, you're not a liar too, are you?

1281
00:54:58.750 --> 00:55:01.950
Her response was like, like proud.

1282
00:55:02.230 --> 00:55:03.950
I've been known to bend the truth.

1283
00:55:04.130 --> 00:55:04.630
I know.

1284
00:55:04.830 --> 00:55:05.970
Like, where's this headed?

1285
00:55:06.430 --> 00:55:07.830
I'm a little sassy.

1286
00:55:08.170 --> 00:55:12.650
Well, Halstead shared with us that Charlie Grandy came up with the Pam, Pam, Pam joke.

1287
00:55:12.910 --> 00:55:15.910
And he said they were trying to think of a funny out to that scene.

1288
00:55:16.070 --> 00:55:21.850
And Charlie came up with the guys running into another woman named Pam who was known to bend the truth.

1289
00:55:21.850 --> 00:55:25.630
Halstead said it always made him laugh to think of this random encounter.

1290
00:55:26.250 --> 00:55:30.010
The woman who played the extra Pam was Sarah Zimmerman.

1291
00:55:30.330 --> 00:55:35.750
She's an accomplished theater actress and has appeared on The Closer, Big Love, and Desperate Housewives.

1292
00:55:36.030 --> 00:55:39.510
And she is an Audie-nominated audiobook narrator.

1293
00:55:39.770 --> 00:55:43.110
She has narrated over 300 audiobooks.

1294
00:55:43.250 --> 00:55:43.710
Wow.

1295
00:55:44.090 --> 00:55:44.250
Yeah.

1296
00:55:44.410 --> 00:55:45.910
I thought she stole the scene.

1297
00:55:46.130 --> 00:55:47.510
Stole the episode, really.

1298
00:55:47.670 --> 00:55:48.510
It's so memorable.

1299
00:55:48.690 --> 00:55:49.390
It really is.

1300
00:55:49.470 --> 00:55:51.190
I had one background catch here.

1301
00:55:51.190 --> 00:55:54.230
If you notice, they're in Nate's blue pickup truck.

1302
00:55:54.370 --> 00:55:54.670
Yes.

1303
00:55:54.870 --> 00:55:56.370
It has an extended cab.

1304
00:55:56.650 --> 00:56:00.570
And other than Sarah's brilliant performance, the other thing I kept thinking about was,

1305
00:56:00.690 --> 00:56:03.910
how did our camera operator fit into that tiny back seat?

1306
00:56:03.970 --> 00:56:05.210
It's not a full back seat.

1307
00:56:05.390 --> 00:56:08.710
And at the end of the scene, Dwight looks over his shoulder.

1308
00:56:08.710 --> 00:56:11.530
So you know our camera operator was right there.

1309
00:56:11.830 --> 00:56:14.250
Well, it was not just our camera operator.

1310
00:56:14.530 --> 00:56:17.050
It was also director Charles McDougal.

1311
00:56:17.150 --> 00:56:19.130
They were both wedged in that little space.

1312
00:56:19.130 --> 00:56:25.370
Matt Sohn and Charles McDougal were in the back seat, and our sound guy, Ben Patrick,

1313
00:56:25.730 --> 00:56:31.490
rigged up microphones that were hardwired into the vehicle, and they went to a sound recorder

1314
00:56:31.490 --> 00:56:33.550
that was mounted in the back of the truck.

1315
00:56:33.930 --> 00:56:37.450
Before we move on, lady, I have a fan catch from Alexander W.

1316
00:56:37.630 --> 00:56:38.990
in Annapolis, Maryland.

1317
00:56:39.430 --> 00:56:40.450
It's a good one.

1318
00:56:40.650 --> 00:56:42.550
I looked, and Alexander is right.

1319
00:56:43.050 --> 00:56:48.530
After Dwight and Nate drive away from the fake office building, you can see a police

1320
00:56:48.530 --> 00:56:52.290
officer on a motorcycle blocking off side streets of traffic.

1321
00:56:52.490 --> 00:56:53.510
Oh, good catch!

1322
00:56:53.870 --> 00:56:54.050
Yeah.

1323
00:56:54.230 --> 00:56:57.350
That's what they would do when we would have to drive through an intersection.

1324
00:56:57.350 --> 00:56:59.050
They would block the traffic for us.

1325
00:56:59.470 --> 00:57:01.710
Back at the office, Dwight's on to Pam.

1326
00:57:01.930 --> 00:57:04.150
He's going to start turning the wheels.

1327
00:57:04.310 --> 00:57:05.030
Is that what they call it?

1328
00:57:05.330 --> 00:57:06.070
Squeezing the wheels?

1329
00:57:06.230 --> 00:57:07.110
You twist the wheels.

1330
00:57:07.250 --> 00:57:07.990
Grease the wheel?

1331
00:57:08.450 --> 00:57:09.770
You twist the knob.

1332
00:57:10.210 --> 00:57:10.290
No.

1333
00:57:10.450 --> 00:57:10.890
What are we?

1334
00:57:11.070 --> 00:57:12.210
What are you talking about?

1335
00:57:12.210 --> 00:57:13.030
Turn the screws!

1336
00:57:15.920 --> 00:57:17.000
Sam, don't even.

1337
00:57:17.540 --> 00:57:19.660
Sam is just looking at me, shaking his head.

1338
00:57:19.740 --> 00:57:20.720
What did you think it was?

1339
00:57:20.860 --> 00:57:22.220
Turn the wheels on Pam?

1340
00:57:22.640 --> 00:57:23.380
Turn the wheels?

1341
00:57:23.640 --> 00:57:24.300
Squeeze the knobs.

1342
00:57:25.200 --> 00:57:26.420
What is happening?

1343
00:57:26.640 --> 00:57:27.360
I don't know.

1344
00:57:27.580 --> 00:57:28.100
Turn the screws.

1345
00:57:28.280 --> 00:57:28.940
Tighten the screws.

1346
00:57:29.220 --> 00:57:34.460
He's tightening the screws, and he's really questioning her about the specs of the space.

1347
00:57:34.460 --> 00:57:35.540
What's the square footage?

1348
00:57:36.020 --> 00:57:37.080
Where's the one sheet?

1349
00:57:37.680 --> 00:57:38.740
She's a funny guy.

1350
00:57:40.160 --> 00:57:41.160
That's what he says.

1351
00:57:41.380 --> 00:57:42.780
He says, Pam, you're a funny guy.

1352
00:57:43.140 --> 00:57:44.480
Oh, I missed that.

1353
00:57:44.660 --> 00:57:46.380
Oh my God, it's such a great moment!

1354
00:57:46.780 --> 00:57:50.580
Well, we had a couple of people write in with a fan catch that involves you, Angela.

1355
00:57:50.940 --> 00:57:51.880
It's delicious.

1356
00:57:52.520 --> 00:57:53.200
Nick N.

1357
00:57:53.420 --> 00:57:54.660
from Minnesota and Nicole W.

1358
00:57:54.940 --> 00:57:58.400
from Bowie, Maryland, both spotted that at 15 minutes

1359
00:57:58.400 --> 00:58:03.720
and 51 seconds, after Dwight tells Pam that he's the only one she hasn't asked about

1360
00:58:03.720 --> 00:58:08.440
the new office building, and Pam replies, I know, that's because you're the reason we're

1361
00:58:08.440 --> 00:58:08.920
moving.

1362
00:58:09.700 --> 00:58:16.780
Over my shoulder, in the background, as the camera zooms in on Pam, Angela can be seen quickly

1363
00:58:16.780 --> 00:58:22.040
changing her computer screen and looking guiltily toward the camera as though she didn't realize

1364
00:58:22.040 --> 00:58:23.300
they'd started shooting.

1365
00:58:23.940 --> 00:58:25.280
And it's true.

1366
00:58:26.600 --> 00:58:27.740
Oh, busted.

1367
00:58:28.680 --> 00:58:33.120
Another thing would have happened right here, it would have been the second mention of Jim's

1368
00:58:33.120 --> 00:58:34.300
rubber band ball.

1369
00:58:34.520 --> 00:58:38.520
In the early drafts, it read, Act 3, Interior Office.

1370
00:58:38.700 --> 00:58:40.100
Dwight and Pam were at their desks.

1371
00:58:40.460 --> 00:58:46.580
Jim playing with his rubber band ball, which is now, and then in all caps, significantly larger.

1372
00:58:46.580 --> 00:58:50.500
So, I remember from my youth, these rubber band balls.

1373
00:58:50.780 --> 00:58:52.080
But here's my question.

1374
00:58:52.520 --> 00:58:56.260
Why did we have so many rubber bands that we could make a ball?

1375
00:58:56.460 --> 00:58:57.600
What was being banded?

1376
00:58:58.020 --> 00:59:02.660
I don't feel like I come across that many rubber bands now in my adult life.

1377
00:59:02.960 --> 00:59:04.220
But do you remember?

1378
00:59:04.480 --> 00:59:04.760
Yes.

1379
00:59:04.880 --> 00:59:05.960
People would make these.

1380
00:59:06.100 --> 00:59:06.400
Yes.

1381
00:59:06.520 --> 00:59:08.900
It was a way to store your rubber bands efficiently.

1382
00:59:09.300 --> 00:59:12.260
But what was coming rubber banded?

1383
00:59:12.460 --> 00:59:13.220
I don't know.

1384
00:59:13.560 --> 00:59:16.360
The newspaper, I think, is the only thing I have that's rubber banded.

1385
00:59:16.580 --> 00:59:19.000
But clearly, Jim is bored, right?

1386
00:59:19.160 --> 00:59:21.320
He's spending the day building a rubber band ball.

1387
00:59:21.500 --> 00:59:22.940
And playing zombie soccer.

1388
00:59:23.180 --> 00:59:25.220
We did establish that he had met his sales quota.

1389
00:59:25.400 --> 00:59:25.600
Yeah.

1390
00:59:25.740 --> 00:59:27.720
So, maybe this is a continuation of that boredom.

1391
00:59:27.820 --> 00:59:28.540
I think so.

1392
00:59:28.880 --> 00:59:32.640
We're going to have a little secret meetup of Jim and Pam in the stairwell.

1393
00:59:33.100 --> 00:59:34.880
Pam is freaking out.

1394
00:59:35.300 --> 00:59:38.480
She confesses to Jim that she lied about the new building.

1395
00:59:38.720 --> 00:59:40.000
It doesn't exiSt.

1396
00:59:40.500 --> 00:59:42.000
Not even the bike path.

1397
00:59:42.560 --> 00:59:43.040
Yes.

1398
00:59:43.440 --> 00:59:46.180
Jim is also going to find out that Pam didn't use his camera.

1399
00:59:46.580 --> 00:59:49.060
I would have liked to have some reaction there.

1400
00:59:49.280 --> 00:59:49.940
Oh, yeah.

1401
00:59:50.100 --> 00:59:50.720
That's true.

1402
00:59:51.200 --> 00:59:56.500
Well, this is when she says she really, really needs to win this.

1403
00:59:56.660 --> 00:59:57.000
Yeah.

1404
00:59:57.320 --> 01:00:00.200
Because, you know, she failed art school.

1405
01:00:00.400 --> 01:00:01.240
She failed sales.

1406
01:00:02.240 --> 01:00:04.520
And Jim says, what are you talking about?

1407
01:00:04.520 --> 01:00:05.360
You didn't fail.

1408
01:00:05.860 --> 01:00:08.220
And I loved this line when they wrote it.

1409
01:00:08.320 --> 01:00:09.540
And I love delivering it.

1410
01:00:09.660 --> 01:00:11.440
She says, well, I'm not an artiSt.

1411
01:00:11.950 --> 01:00:13.260
And I'm not a salesman.

1412
01:00:13.500 --> 01:00:14.820
So, what would you call it?

1413
01:00:15.920 --> 01:00:22.500
And we get kind of a glimpse into maybe just some sadness that Pam carries around.

1414
01:00:22.800 --> 01:00:27.340
And also, how much this position that she created, by the way, really means to her.

1415
01:00:27.500 --> 01:00:28.400
She needs a win.

1416
01:00:28.400 --> 01:00:30.660
We had a fan question from Gabby C.

1417
01:00:30.680 --> 01:00:32.540
in Huntsville, Alabama, who said,

1418
01:00:32.700 --> 01:00:33.700
Question for Jenna.

1419
01:00:33.960 --> 01:00:37.460
In this episode, Pam talks about failing at things.

1420
01:00:38.080 --> 01:00:43.760
I'm wondering, does Pam think she failed at being a receptionist, too, since she no longer has that job?

1421
01:00:43.980 --> 01:00:48.400
I'm just curious what her thinking and motivation behind that line is.

1422
01:00:48.400 --> 01:00:54.560
Well, Gabby, I did not feel like Pam felt she failed at being a receptioniSt.

1423
01:00:54.760 --> 01:01:03.500
But I always thought that taking the job as a receptionist was something she felt she had to do.

1424
01:01:03.800 --> 01:01:05.680
It wasn't maybe in her heart.

1425
01:01:05.820 --> 01:01:11.740
She has that line early on where she says, not many little girls, you know, dream of growing up and being a receptioniSt.

1426
01:01:11.940 --> 01:01:16.020
You know, it's sort of like what you do when you become an adult and you have responsibilities.

1427
01:01:16.020 --> 01:01:18.680
But it certainly wasn't her passion in life.

1428
01:01:18.840 --> 01:01:30.660
But, yeah, I think she's always carried around this feeling, just this longing to express herself in some way artistically.

1429
01:01:31.160 --> 01:01:36.440
And, you know, she left art school because she didn't want to get into graphic design.

1430
01:01:36.440 --> 01:01:39.780
That wasn't how she wanted to express her artistry.

1431
01:01:39.780 --> 01:01:45.620
But she certainly hasn't really spent a lot of effort figuring it out lately.

1432
01:01:46.260 --> 01:01:48.480
And I think that's coming up for her.

1433
01:01:48.580 --> 01:01:54.740
And I would remind myself of that as the show went on, that this was the thing that had gone unexpressed for her.

1434
01:01:55.220 --> 01:01:55.400
Right.

1435
01:01:55.700 --> 01:01:58.080
We're going to head over to Caffeine Corner now.

1436
01:01:58.620 --> 01:01:59.000
Mm-hmm.

1437
01:01:59.440 --> 01:02:02.620
There would have been a third mention of the rubber band ball here.

1438
01:02:02.920 --> 01:02:04.560
Well, it's much larger.

1439
01:02:04.560 --> 01:02:09.480
And he's banding the whole time that Michael and Oscar are debating China.

1440
01:02:09.480 --> 01:02:10.040
Mm-hmm.

1441
01:02:10.400 --> 01:02:16.500
In an earlier draft, it would have said, Jim, Andy, Kelly, Ryan, and Aaron sit nearby around a small round table.

1442
01:02:16.960 --> 01:02:21.160
Oscar turns to them and says, I didn't realize this place was so popular.

1443
01:02:21.380 --> 01:02:24.380
Kelly and Ryan mime being busy and in conversation.

1444
01:02:25.040 --> 01:02:28.820
Aaron puts her hands to her ears pretending to groove to music.

1445
01:02:29.500 --> 01:02:32.840
Andy pretends to eat a sandwich but doesn't take a bite.

1446
01:02:32.840 --> 01:02:34.600
I want you to watch the whole scene.

1447
01:02:34.980 --> 01:02:38.020
Ed hovers over that sandwich and never bites down.

1448
01:02:38.840 --> 01:02:42.940
And Jim is playing with his now bigger rubber band ball.

1449
01:02:43.220 --> 01:02:46.780
Well, you know, at first, it looks like Michael's doing pretty well.

1450
01:02:47.620 --> 01:02:49.960
He's got some good China facts.

1451
01:02:50.240 --> 01:02:50.580
Mm-hmm.

1452
01:02:50.920 --> 01:02:54.180
But ultimately, Oscar's going to start steamrolling him.

1453
01:02:54.560 --> 01:03:02.360
At 18 minutes, five seconds, there is a great shot from Hank's point of view looking at Michael and Oscar as they chat.

1454
01:03:02.360 --> 01:03:08.120
And I want you to know you can see two boxes of the game Call of Duty under Hank's desk.

1455
01:03:09.080 --> 01:03:11.580
Well, you have a good friend in Matt C.

1456
01:03:11.700 --> 01:03:12.960
from Virginia who knows the same thing.

1457
01:03:12.960 --> 01:03:13.880
Matt, did you see those?

1458
01:03:14.200 --> 01:03:14.400
Yes.

1459
01:03:14.800 --> 01:03:19.100
You know, you can also see paper bags and napkins, I think, for to-go iteMs.

1460
01:03:19.380 --> 01:03:20.080
I saw that.

1461
01:03:20.200 --> 01:03:21.800
And the paper bags made sense.

1462
01:03:21.980 --> 01:03:22.940
Napkins made sense.

1463
01:03:23.000 --> 01:03:25.180
And then I'm like, what's the two boxes of Call of Duty?

1464
01:03:25.180 --> 01:03:34.300
Also, if you look at the sign in the lobby behind Andy and Jim, you know, the little sign that says what all of the different offices are?

1465
01:03:34.400 --> 01:03:34.680
Mm-hmm.

1466
01:03:34.840 --> 01:03:36.640
The sign in the lobby now reads,

1467
01:03:37.140 --> 01:03:41.420
The Schrute Building under new management below Vance Refrigeration.

1468
01:03:41.620 --> 01:03:45.200
A lot of fun little background catches in this scene with Michael and Oscar.

1469
01:03:45.420 --> 01:03:53.280
But ultimately, Michael is going to prevail by giving a speech about how it's good to just keep talking.

1470
01:03:54.120 --> 01:03:54.600
Yes.

1471
01:03:54.860 --> 01:04:01.980
And everyone's going to get really enthusiastic and riled up, and they're going to love America at the end.

1472
01:04:02.160 --> 01:04:02.580
Yes.

1473
01:04:02.800 --> 01:04:08.840
Michael is going to do the thing he does, you guys, where he says crazy things with a lot of enthusiasm.

1474
01:04:09.240 --> 01:04:09.980
He says,

1475
01:04:10.140 --> 01:04:13.280
Many people say you will get to China if you dig long and hard enough.

1476
01:04:13.420 --> 01:04:19.980
But what they don't tell you is that if you dig long and hard enough in a conversation, you get to a friend.

1477
01:04:20.600 --> 01:04:20.760
Yeah.

1478
01:04:20.760 --> 01:04:23.120
Everyone cheers, case closed.

1479
01:04:23.480 --> 01:04:28.540
Did you notice everyone leaves and goes up the stairs and not a single person gets in the elevator?

1480
01:04:29.140 --> 01:04:30.400
No, I did not.

1481
01:04:31.020 --> 01:04:32.040
Elevator's right there.

1482
01:04:32.440 --> 01:04:33.760
No one uses it.

1483
01:04:33.900 --> 01:04:35.520
Everyone's getting their steps in.

1484
01:04:35.640 --> 01:04:36.000
Mm-hmm.

1485
01:04:36.000 --> 01:04:46.980
I remember how excited we were to get those tables and chairs that remained there in the lobby after this episode because we finally had a place to sit down whenever we were shooting over in that area.

1486
01:04:47.100 --> 01:04:48.420
Yeah, we'd actually hang out there.

1487
01:04:48.420 --> 01:04:53.880
There was a deleted Oscar talking head that would have followed Michael's big speech.

1488
01:04:54.640 --> 01:04:56.540
Oscar cannot believe what just happened.

1489
01:04:56.840 --> 01:04:58.000
I thought we should hear it.

1490
01:04:58.480 --> 01:05:01.120
Once I pay the coffee, that's the coup de gras.

1491
01:05:01.360 --> 01:05:01.800
It's over.

1492
01:05:01.980 --> 01:05:04.460
You can't make a 180-degree change of position.

1493
01:05:04.900 --> 01:05:06.500
How could he possibly think he won?

1494
01:05:07.560 --> 01:05:09.340
I don't know how to deal with tactics like that.

1495
01:05:11.220 --> 01:05:11.680
Nice.

1496
01:05:12.060 --> 01:05:17.260
But it also proves that Oscar knows exactly what he's doing when he invites you for a cup of coffee.

1497
01:05:17.600 --> 01:05:19.980
Which is exactly what Jim and Andy said.

1498
01:05:20.160 --> 01:05:20.500
Mm-hmm.

1499
01:05:20.500 --> 01:05:24.060
We had a fun fan question from Tibby H.

1500
01:05:24.360 --> 01:05:25.160
in Belgium.

1501
01:05:25.900 --> 01:05:28.360
Was Oscar actually the smartest on set?

1502
01:05:30.500 --> 01:05:33.620
I'll tell you who I think the smartest people on set were.

1503
01:05:33.620 --> 01:05:34.760
Oh, I have my thoughts, too.

1504
01:05:34.900 --> 01:05:36.060
Oh, can we say them at the same time?

1505
01:05:36.140 --> 01:05:36.360
Okay.

1506
01:05:36.720 --> 01:05:36.880
Okay.

1507
01:05:37.360 --> 01:05:37.800
Mindy.

1508
01:05:37.920 --> 01:05:38.220
Mindy and BJ.

1509
01:05:38.420 --> 01:05:38.600
Ah!

1510
01:05:38.940 --> 01:05:39.380
Yeah.

1511
01:05:39.480 --> 01:05:39.680
BJ.

1512
01:05:40.320 --> 01:05:41.060
Mike Scherr.

1513
01:05:41.280 --> 01:05:41.700
Paul.

1514
01:05:42.160 --> 01:05:42.480
Yeah.

1515
01:05:43.040 --> 01:05:44.120
Basically, our writers.

1516
01:05:44.420 --> 01:05:44.740
Mm-hmm.

1517
01:05:44.940 --> 01:05:47.820
Our writers were all so, so smart.

1518
01:05:48.440 --> 01:05:52.460
But Mindy and BJ, I mean, I have shared, sometimes I would walk up to them just to be like,

1519
01:05:52.520 --> 01:05:53.200
hey, what's going on?

1520
01:05:53.280 --> 01:05:58.320
And they would be in such a thoughtful, intellectual conversation that I would just slowly back away.

1521
01:05:58.820 --> 01:06:00.860
I don't know how to add in.

1522
01:06:01.180 --> 01:06:03.040
I would just stand there and listen and stare at them and stuff.

1523
01:06:03.260 --> 01:06:03.440
Yeah.

1524
01:06:04.020 --> 01:06:10.480
While all of this is going on in Caffeine Corner, Pam is going to get a little secret package from Nate.

1525
01:06:10.840 --> 01:06:11.840
Yeah, he wasn't there.

1526
01:06:12.040 --> 01:06:12.620
He wasn't there.

1527
01:06:12.740 --> 01:06:13.360
And she's like, what?

1528
01:06:13.420 --> 01:06:14.120
And he goes, it's a phrase.

1529
01:06:14.240 --> 01:06:14.800
People use it.

1530
01:06:14.880 --> 01:06:16.360
Feel free to use this phrase.

1531
01:06:16.360 --> 01:06:17.200
Yeah, look into it.

1532
01:06:17.200 --> 01:06:18.260
Because it could be helpful.

1533
01:06:18.560 --> 01:06:19.800
My God, he's so funny.

1534
01:06:20.160 --> 01:06:22.120
We had a fan catch from Abby S.

1535
01:06:22.280 --> 01:06:26.580
from Atlanta, Georgia, who noticed that at 17 minutes and 30 seconds,

1536
01:06:26.740 --> 01:06:29.660
Nate sets the book down in front of Pam on the counter.

1537
01:06:29.960 --> 01:06:37.840
But then just a few seconds later, as Nate is walking away, suddenly the book is in Pam's hand and it's open.

1538
01:06:38.260 --> 01:06:40.400
It seems like, when did that happen?

1539
01:06:40.520 --> 01:06:41.380
It's a good catch.

1540
01:06:41.900 --> 01:06:44.420
Well, Pam is going to read something in this book.

1541
01:06:44.420 --> 01:06:48.740
Look, it's going to give her an edge and she's going to march right into Dwight's office.

1542
01:06:49.060 --> 01:06:50.680
Yeah, she's going to say you're breaking the law.

1543
01:06:50.920 --> 01:06:55.240
And she's got all the things that a landlord is supposed to provide in a workspace.

1544
01:06:55.880 --> 01:06:57.360
And Dwight's not doing that.

1545
01:06:57.840 --> 01:06:58.140
Yeah.

1546
01:06:58.520 --> 01:06:59.700
And she's right.

1547
01:06:59.700 --> 01:07:02.800
And he has to put everything back the way it was.

1548
01:07:02.960 --> 01:07:03.780
She wins it.

1549
01:07:03.960 --> 01:07:04.280
Yeah.

1550
01:07:04.520 --> 01:07:09.000
The episode ends with them going outside and they're taking the big banner down.

1551
01:07:09.000 --> 01:07:10.560
And she's so proud of herself.

1552
01:07:10.880 --> 01:07:13.060
There was a Dwight talking head that says,

1553
01:07:13.180 --> 01:07:15.660
the best vampires don't bleed their victims dry,

1554
01:07:15.660 --> 01:07:18.700
but give them the strength to bounce back and be fed on again.

1555
01:07:18.880 --> 01:07:22.540
And he said he spared Pam, not because he was motivated by compassion.

1556
01:07:23.060 --> 01:07:23.680
Not at all.

1557
01:07:24.000 --> 01:07:24.280
Yeah.

1558
01:07:24.400 --> 01:07:24.900
But he spared her.

1559
01:07:25.040 --> 01:07:25.860
He let her win.

1560
01:07:26.240 --> 01:07:26.500
Yeah.

1561
01:07:26.820 --> 01:07:31.820
During this talking head, there's a little bit of like a flashback of extra footage.

1562
01:07:31.820 --> 01:07:36.480
We go back to that stairwell and the camera pans up and you see that Dwight has overheard

1563
01:07:36.480 --> 01:07:38.700
this whole exchange between Jim and Pam.

1564
01:07:38.920 --> 01:07:41.880
And he knows how badly Pam needs this win.

1565
01:07:42.120 --> 01:07:42.520
Mm-hmm.

1566
01:07:42.960 --> 01:07:47.380
There was a candy bag alt for this Dwight talking head and it read like this.

1567
01:07:47.500 --> 01:07:48.960
And I really liked it.

1568
01:07:49.240 --> 01:07:50.620
B-roll spy shot.

1569
01:07:50.820 --> 01:07:54.220
Dwight is in his car looking at Pam in the rear view mirror.

1570
01:07:54.580 --> 01:08:00.580
She seems happy and Dwight's face betrays the slightest hint of human warmth.

1571
01:08:00.580 --> 01:08:04.060
Over this spy shot, we hear a Dwight talking head.

1572
01:08:04.500 --> 01:08:06.700
Dwight says, she's clean.

1573
01:08:07.020 --> 01:08:07.960
She's polite.

1574
01:08:08.300 --> 01:08:11.200
She doesn't chat your ear off about medical dramas.

1575
01:08:12.060 --> 01:08:15.920
Sometimes when Jim is being a tool, we'll roll our eyes at each other.

1576
01:08:16.520 --> 01:08:20.120
In a way, she performs many of the functions of a friend.

1577
01:08:20.640 --> 01:08:25.420
And then he goes on to say, so if someone had to beat me, I guess I'm glad it was her.

1578
01:08:25.800 --> 01:08:26.760
Don't get me wrong.

1579
01:08:26.760 --> 01:08:28.820
I'm really angry about that.

1580
01:08:29.000 --> 01:08:29.500
Grr.

1581
01:08:29.500 --> 01:08:32.280
I wish that had been the talking head.

1582
01:08:32.480 --> 01:08:33.840
Wasn't that so sweet?

1583
01:08:33.840 --> 01:08:35.000
It gave me chills.

1584
01:08:35.220 --> 01:08:35.780
I know.

1585
01:08:36.420 --> 01:08:40.240
Well, this episode ends with another win, this time for Andy.

1586
01:08:40.840 --> 01:08:42.620
He has texted Daryl.

1587
01:08:43.220 --> 01:08:46.880
There's some crazy pigeon stuff happening in the parking lot.

1588
01:08:47.340 --> 01:08:47.720
Yeah.

1589
01:08:48.020 --> 01:08:51.300
There's two pigeons and they're eating an ice cream cone.

1590
01:08:51.620 --> 01:08:55.100
In the script, it said they had ice cream all over their little faces.

1591
01:08:55.840 --> 01:08:58.520
We had a fan question from Sarah C.

1592
01:08:58.580 --> 01:08:59.600
in Lubbock, Texas.

1593
01:09:00.000 --> 01:09:02.400
How did the scene with the pigeons happen?

1594
01:09:02.680 --> 01:09:04.020
Are they trained pigeons?

1595
01:09:04.280 --> 01:09:06.680
And how much does a trained pigeon cost?

1596
01:09:06.680 --> 01:09:10.280
Well, Sarah, Randy Cordray has an answer for you.

1597
01:09:10.540 --> 01:09:14.780
We got those pigeons from Bob Dunn's Animal Rentals in Sylmar, California.

1598
01:09:15.460 --> 01:09:18.120
Denise Sanders was our head pigeon trainer.

1599
01:09:18.120 --> 01:09:25.880
And it cost us $2,600 for two trainers and three pigeons, even though we only used two.

1600
01:09:26.160 --> 01:09:28.140
How the heck do you train a pigeon?

1601
01:09:28.480 --> 01:09:29.460
I don't know.

1602
01:09:29.660 --> 01:09:31.700
But Bob Dunn, they know how to do it.

1603
01:09:31.820 --> 01:09:32.840
They can train anything.

1604
01:09:33.500 --> 01:09:36.620
So that made me laugh.

1605
01:09:36.900 --> 01:09:38.060
I don't know why.

1606
01:09:38.280 --> 01:09:38.820
Made me laugh.

1607
01:09:39.460 --> 01:09:40.820
Well, that was Chyna.

1608
01:09:41.020 --> 01:09:45.380
Big thank you to Warren and Halstead for sharing with us about writing this episode.

1609
01:09:45.660 --> 01:09:48.660
Randy Cordray for all the great behind-the-scenes details.

1610
01:09:48.940 --> 01:09:50.820
And thank you again for all your questions.

1611
01:09:51.160 --> 01:09:51.660
Yeah.

1612
